    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d499">

                <pb facs="0483" n="473" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg473"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/1–749: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Peiping</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Clubb</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-01-07T18:00:00-05:00">January 7, 1949—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8. Dept requests info Fu Tso-yi’s<anchor n="6" xml:id="d499-f1"/> disposition US
                    arms delivered Nov from CinCFE.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d499-f2"/> Were arms
                    retained Peiping–Tientsin area or sent west?</p>
                <p>Dept interested ConGen’s estimate whether food stocks Peiping and possible
                    channels supply would enable city endure extended investment.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lovett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d499-f1" n="6" xml:id="d499-f1-content">
                <p>Commander in Chief of the North China Bandit Suppression Headquarters.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d499-f2" n="7" xml:id="d499-f2-content">
                <p>Commander in Chief, Far East (MacArthur).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
